\name{ImplementationShortfallOrder}
\alias{ImplementationShortfallOrder}
\title{A time-sliced order whose slices shrink, so most of it trades early}
\description{
Trading early keeps the price paid close to the price when the decision was made, at the cost of more market impact. At an \code{urgency} of 0 it is exactly a \code{TimeWeightedAveragePriceOrder}. By default UBI holds each slice in its virtual order book from its turn until the other side of the book reaches its price, unless \code{hold_limits} is \code{FALSE}, but only when the template is a \code{limit} order with a price that is neither \code{ioc} nor after-market; any other order, such as a market TWAP, is sent unheld, slice by slice. Slices are shared out in whole lots, and a slice that comes to nothing, as it does when the order has fewer lots than slices, is skipped and the schedule moves on.

The order template's attributes are described on \code{SyntheticOrder}.
}
